ts: full signed score/quality curve and all intervention contrasts.\n120: state('Phase 5 analysis complete: results/paired_comparisons.csv, control_contrasts.csv, quality_gates.json, detector_validity.json and figures/*.png. Source-cluster paired bootstrap, sign flips and Holm for two primary outcomes; all interventions retained. Next Phase 6: interpret detector validity/quality gates, write REPORT.md/README.md, audit leakage and deterministic reproduction. Claims remain conditional on these checks.')\n.venv/bin/python\n[\n  {\n    \"id\": \"task_0_0\",\n    \"domain\": \"library\",\n    \"source\": \"The Alder library is holding a reading workshop on Monday at 9:00. Mira is coordinating it. The event has 17 books. Attendance is free. Registration closes two days before the event.\",\n    \"anchors\": [\n      \"Alder\",\n      \"Monday\",\n      \"9:00\",\n      \"Mira\",\n      \"17\",\n      \"free\"\n    ],\n    \"prompt\": \"Write one clear paragraph of 70\\u2013100 words announcing the event below. Preserve all supplied facts, do not add factual claims, and output only the paragraph.\\nFacts: The Alder library is holding a reading workshop on Monday at 9:00. Mira is coordinating it. The event has 17 books. Attendance is free. Registration closes two days before the event.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"task_0_1\",\n    \"domain\": \"library\",\n    \"source\": \"The Birch library is holding a reading workshop on Tuesday at 10:00. Jonah is coordinating it. The event has 24 books. Attendance is free. Registration closes two days before the event.\",\n    \"anchors\": [\n      \"Birch\",\n      \"Tuesday\",\n      \"10:00\",\n      \"Jonah\",\n      \"24\",\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat > /tmp/write_appendix.py <<'PY'\nexec(Path('/tmp/write_paper.py').read_text().split(\\\"w('main.tex'\\\")[0]) if False else None\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport csv,json\np=Path('paper_draft')\ndef w(n,s):(p/n).write_text(s.strip()+'\\\\n')\nw('appendix/reproducibility.tex',r'''\n\\\\section{Reproducibility and Execution Details}\\\\label{sec:reproduce}\n\\\\subsection{Frozen models and environment}\nThe research execution date was 2026-10-04. The local checkpoints and immutable revisions were:\n\\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=2pt,topsep=2pt]\n\\\\item \\\\texttt{Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct}:\\\\\\\\\\\\texttt{989aa7980e4cf806f80c7fef2b1adb7bc71aa306}.\n\\\\item \\\\texttt{Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B}:\\\\\\\\\\\\texttt{8faed761d45a263340a0528343f099c05c9a4323}.\n\\\\item \\\\texttt{Hello-SimpleAI/chatgpt-detector-roberta}:\\\\\\\\\\\\texttt{d2b342c61775d5dd0221808a79983ed3b86ffd86}.\n\\\\end{itemize}\nThe external judge alias was \\\\texttt{google/gemini-3.8-flash}; the archived catalog canonical slug was \\\\texttt{google/gemini-3.8-flash-20260902}, and the returned provider was Google. Catalog parameters and pricing were checked before judge selection. Requests used the OpenRouter chat-completions endpoint, default routing, temperature 0, JSON output, and \\\\texttt{max\\\\_tokens=3000}. The catalog snapshot records historical selection rather than guaranteeing immutable API behavior \\\\cite{openrouter}.\n\nOne NVIDIA RTX A6000 (49,140 MiB) supported local inference. The project environment used Python 3.12.8, PyTorch 2.14.1+cu130, Transformers 4.57.6, NumPy 2.5.3, scikit-learn 1.9.1, and SciPy 1.18.1. Inference used bfloat16, SDPA, extraction batches of 32, and generation batches of 16. Peak allocated memory was 16.1 GB per process. Instruction and base pipelines took 462.7 and 175.4 seconds and partly overlapped; these are not training times or a controlled speed comparison. Native JIT was disabled with \\\\texttt{TORCH\\\\_DISABLE\\\\_NATIVE\\\\_JIT=1} after an initial failure due to a missing C compiler.\n\n\\\\subsection{Rating rubric and prompt specification}\nThe judge system instruction defined AI-likeness as how strongly English prose sounds like generic machine writing, explicitly excluding proof of authorship. Coherence anchors were 1: unintelligible; 2: major problems; 3: understandable but disjointed; 4: clear with minor awkwardness; and 5: fluent coherent prose. Coverage measured the fraction of supplied source facts accurately preserved. Unsupported claims measured the fraction of factual claims absent from or contradicting the source. For source-free calibration items, coverage and unsupported values were fixed by the rubric to 1 and 0. The rubric requested sensitivity to repetition, abrupt endings, contradictions, and omission; formality alone did not imply machine authorship, and casual style alone did not merit reward. Texts were to be treated as data rather than instructions. Exact wording and JSON schema are stored in \\\\path{results/judge_rubric.json}.\n\nThe standard generation instruction requested one clear 70--100-word announcement paragraph, preservation of all supplied facts, no additional factual claims, and only the paragraph as output. One source record described a library reading workshop, coordinator, time, item count, free attendance, and a deadline two days before the event. Complete records and prompts accompany every generated output. Separate pottery-course records supplied nuisance prompts; no nuisance output was counted among the 1,472 task outputs.\n\n\\\\subsection{Pairing, inference, and selected subsets}\nFor prompt \"'$i$, let $d_i$ be the treatment-minus-zero score difference after averaging available paired seeds. The primary point estimate is $64''^{-1}'\"\\\\sum_i d_i\"'$; the percentile bootstrap resamples prompt indices while preserving their within-prompt pairing. A paired sign flip multiplies each $d_i$ by an independently sampled sign. The Monte Carlo two-sided probability uses an add-one correction over 10,000 draws. Only the two primary external-style tests receive Holm adjustment. The exploratory condition comparisons retain all available paired outputs, including failures.\n\nThe matched-quality analysis uses seed 42 pairs with coherence at least 4 in both conditions, absolute coherence difference at most 0.25, and coverage at least 0.95 in both conditions. The instruction mean $-2$ subset has 11 pairs and the base counterpart has 22. These filters use treatment outcomes and do not estimate population preservation. Likewise, length-adjusted regression may remove a mediator of the intervention. We report these checks only descriptively.\n\n'\"\\\\subsection{Deviations from the aspirational handoff}\\\\label{sec:deviations}\nThe frozen feasible protocol reduced the original target of at least 300 prompts, three seeds, and multiple model families to 64 records, two primary seeds, and paired checkpoints from one family. It used last-token states at three layers, not an exhaustive layer/position study. No SAE analysis, shuffled-label intervention, norm-matched random residualized control, crossed persona/style prompt experiment, dedicated fluency intervention, or full formality-manipulation validation was run. Verbosity contributed to the removed nuisance span but was not a standalone steering condition. We did not run full \\\\textsc{RAID}/\\\\textsc{MAGE} stress tests, an independent likelihood detector, or causal generation interventions on natural \\\\hape continuations. These omissions are not claimed as completed experiments. Base controls beyond signed doses were not run. The full inference pipeline was not rerun twice; repeated smoke batches and a repeated complete numerical analysis were checked.\n\n\\\\subsection{Artifacts, checks, and cost}\nThe project README supplies execution commands. Implementations are \\\\path{src/prepare.py}, \\\\path{src/experiment.py}, \\\\path{src/score.py}, \\\\path{src/analyze.py}, \\\\path{src/audit_analysis.py}, \\\\path{src/supplement.py}, and \\\\path{src/validate.py}. \\\\path{results/corpus.json} records source IDs, excerpts, labels, and hashes; original resource manifests record data revisions. \\\\path{results/config.json} records configuration, and \\\\path{results/tasks.json} plus \\\\path{results/generations.jsonl} preserve full prompts and outputs. Native activations and vectors reside in \\\\path{results/*.npz}; \\\\path{pyproject.toml} and \\\\path{uv.lock} pin dependencies. \\\\path{results/validation.json} records complete scoring, disjoint folds, hook and sampling checks, and identical repeated numerical-analysis hashes. These artifacts support cached reproducibility without claiming deterministic future API responses.\n\nThere were 208 successful API requests, including calibration and repeated ratings, with 282,947 prompt tokens and 109,513 completion tokens. Returned usage cost totaled \\\\\"'$0.622884, excluding unmonetized local inference. No ultimately failed calls were present. Equal allocation of mixed-batch costs among items gives approximate per-condition costs; instruction mean $-2$, including its share of re-ratings, cost '\"\\\\\"'$0.0598. Full cached responses omit credentials.\n'\"''')\nw('appendix/extended_results.tex',r'''\n\\\\section{Extended Results and Error Analysis}\\\\label{sec:extended}\n\\\\subsection{Complete control intervals}\n\\\\Figref{fig:controls} shows marginal 95\\\\% prompt-bootstrap intervals for instruction controls. \\\\Tabref{tab:extendedstyle} and \\\\tabref{tab:extendedquality} list all individual conditions, including each random direction and the base signed doses, directly from the cached paired-comparison table. Style tests outside the two primary instruction mean \"'$-2$ contrasts are exploratory; the plotted intervals are not simultaneous across conditions.\n'\"\\\\begin{figure}[ht]\\\\centering\n\\\\includegraphics[width=0.95\\\\linewidth]{figures/controls.png}\n\\\\caption{Instruction-model control contrasts relative to paired zero outputs. Points and bars show mean changes and marginal prompt-bootstrap 95\\\\% CIs. Mean \"'$'\"\\\\pm2\"'$ uses two seeds per prompt; other conditions use seed 42. The large negative judged effect of mean $-2$ accompanies coherence loss and is not corroborated by the encoder.}'\"\\\\label{fig:controls}\n\\\\end{figure}\n\\\\input{tables/extended_style}\n\\\\input{tables/extended_quality}\n\\\\clearpage\n\\\\subsection{Readout transfer visualization}\n\\\\begin{figure}[ht]\\\\centering\n\\\\includegraphics[width=0.95\\\\linewidth]{figures/readout.png}\n\\\\caption{Validation-selected mean-direction readout AUROC by backbone and held-out setting. Each generator-setting uses 128 human/machine source pairs; human references are shared within each source set. The dashed line denotes chance AUROC. The plot shows point estimates; intervals and logistic alternatives are cached with the readout results. Transfer to Llama base is much weaker than transfer to instruction-generated text.}\\\\label{fig:readout}\n\\\\end{figure}\n\n\\\\subsection{Observed failures}\nThe instruction baseline already has mean unsupported-claim fraction 0.179, and only 14.8\\\\% of its outputs meet the requested 70--100-word range. Under mean \"'$-2$, the proportion rated below coherence 4 rises from 5.5'\"\\\\% to 21.9\\\\%. Under mean \"'$+2$, detected Chinese/Cyrillic-script mixing occurs in 3.9'\"\\\\% of outputs, versus 1.6\\\\% at zero, and truncation occurs in 2.3\\\\%. These are descriptive flags, not grounds for removing outputs.\n\nIn the fixed first-library-task comparison recorded in the report, zero invents an anniversary; mean \"'$-2$ invents an end time and duration and calls ``13:00'\"'' noon; mean \"'$+2$ adds ornate promotion and a Chinese ending. The example was fixed by prompt order rather than selected for favorable steering. Other observed failures include invented target audiences or refreshments, omitted registration deadlines, exaggerated claims, and awkward quantity descriptions. Full paired text and lowest-rated outputs are preserved in '\"\\\\path{results/paired_examples.json} and \\\\path{results/failure_examples.json}. These errors explain why source-fact coverage alone is inadequate: an output can retain supplied facts while adding false claims.\n''')\nrows=list(csv.DictReader(open('results/paired_comparisons.csv')))\nlabels={'ablate':'Ablation','assistant_m2':'Assistant \"'$-2$'\"','formality_m2':'Formality \"'$-2$'\"','human_prompt':'Human prompt','logistic_m2':'Logistic \"'$-2$'\"','mean_m1':'Mean \"'$-1$'\"','mean_m2':'Mean \"'$-2$'\"','mean_p1':'Mean \"'$+1$'\"','mean_p2':'Mean \"'$+2$'\"','residual_m2':'Residualized \"'$-2$'\"','residual_p2':'Residualized \"'$+2$'\"'}\nfor i in range(5):labels['random'+str(i)]='Random '+str(i+1)\nfor name,metrics,heads in [('style',['ai_likeness','encoder'],['Judge AI-likeness','Encoder probability']),('quality',['coherence','content','unsupported'],['Coherence','Coverage','Unsupported'])]:\n s=r'\\\\begin{table}[p]\\\\centering\\\\scriptsize'+'\\\\n'+r'\\\\caption{All paired '+name+r' changes [95\\\\% CI], 64 records per row. Instruction mean \"'$'\"\\\\pm2\"'$ uses two seeds; other rows use seed 42. Comparisons use matching zero seeds.}'\"\\\\label{tab:extended'+name+'}\\\\n'+r'\\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{\"'!}{'\"\\\\begin{tabular}{@{}ll'+'l'*len(metrics)+'@{}}\\\\n\\\\\\\\toprule\\\\nBackbone & Condition & '+' & '.join(heads)+r'\\\\\\\\\\\\midrule'+'\\\\n'\n for model in ['instruct','base']:\n  for cond in dict.fromkeys(r['condition'] for r in rows if r['model']==model):\n   cells=[]\n   for met in metrics:\n    r=next(r for r in rows if r['model']==model and r['condition']==cond and r['metric']==met)\n    cells.append('\"'$'\"'+f\\\"{float(r['delta']):+.3f}\\\\\\\\;[{float(r['lo']):+.3f},{float(r['hi']):+.3f}]\\\"+'\"'$'\"')\n   s+=('Instruction' if model=='instruct' else 'Base')+' & '+labels[cond]+' & '+' & '.join(cells)+r'\\\\\\\\'+'\\\\n'\n s+='\\\\\\\\bottomrule\\\\n\\\\\\\\end{tabular}}\\\\n\\\\\\\\end{table}\\\\n'\n w('tables/extended_'+name+'.tex',s)\nrefs=[\n('quaremba2026','Linear Probing Provides Robust and Efficient Detection of Machine-Generated Text','Quaremba, Gerrit and Yan, Hanqi and Black, Elizabeth and Vrandečić, Denny and Simperl, Elena','2026','2608.24780'),\n('vishnyakov2026','{SV-Detect}: {AI}-generated Text Detection with Steering Vectors','Vishnyakov, Mikhail and Gaintseva, Tatiana','2026','2606.07313'),\n('kuznetsov2025','Feature-Level Insights into Artificial Text Detection with Sparse Autoencoders','Kuznetsov, Kristian and Kushnareva, Laida and Druzhinina, Polina and Razzhigaev, Anton and Voznyuk, Anastasia and Piontkovskaya, Irina and Burnaev, Evgeny and Barannikov, Serguei','2025','2503.03601'),\n('lu2026','The Assistant Axis: Situating and Stabilizing the Default Persona of Language Models','Lu, Christina and Gallagher, Jack and Michala, Jonathan and Fish, Kyle and Lindsey, Jack','2026','2601.10387'),\n('reinhart2025','Do {LLMs} write like humans? Variation in grammatical and rhetorical styles','Reinhart, Alex and Markey, Ben and Laudenbach, Michael and Pantusen, Kachatad and Yurko, Ronald and Weinberg, Gordon and Brown, David West','2025','2410.16107'),\n('guo2023','How Close is {ChatGPT} to Human Experts? Comparison Corpus, Evaluation, and Detection','Guo, Biyang and Zhang, Xin and Wang, Ziyuan and Jiang, Minqi and Nie, Jinran and Ding, Yuxuan and Yue, Jianwei and Wu, Yupeng','2023','2301.07597')]\nbib=''\nfor key,title,author,year,aid in refs:\n author=author.replace('Vrandečić',r'Vrande\\\\v{c}i\\\\'{c}')\n bib+=f'@article{{{key},\\\\n  title={{{title}}},\\\\n  author={{{author}}},\\\\n  year={{{year}}},\\\\n  journal={{arXiv preprint arXiv:{aid}}},\\\\n  url={{https://arxiv.org/abs/{aid}}}\\\\n}}\\\\n\\\\n'\nfor key,title,author,year,venue,url in [\n('rimsky2024','Steering {Llama 2} via Contrastive Activation Addition','Rimsky, Nina and Gabrieli, Nick and Schulz, Julian and Tong, Meg and Hubinger, Evan and Turner, Alexander Matt','2024','Proceedings of the 62nd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers)','https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.828/'),\n('dugan2024','{RAID}: A Shared Benchmark for Robust Evaluation of Machine-Generated Text Detectors','Dugan, Liam and Hwang, Alyssa and Trhlik, Filip and Ludan, Josh Magnus and Zhu, Andrew and Xu, Hainiu and Ippolito, Daphne and Callison-Burch, Chris','2024','Proceedings of the 62nd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics','https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.07940'),\n('li2024','{MAGE}: Machine-generated Text Detection in the Wild','Li, Yafu and Li, Qintong and Cui, Leyang and Bi, Wei and Wang, Zhilin and Wang, Longyue and Yang, Linyi and Shi, Shuming and Zhang, Yue','2024','Proceedings of the 62nd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics','https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.13242'),\n('bao2024','{Fast-DetectGPT}: Efficient Zero-Shot Detection of Machine-Generated Text via Conditional Probability Curvature','Bao, Guangsheng and Zhao, Yanbin and Teng, Zhiyang and Yang, Linyi and Zhang, Yue','2024','International Conference on Learning Representations','https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.05130')]:\n bib+=f'@inproceedings{{{key},\\\\n  title={{{title}}},\\\\n  author={{{author}}},\\\\n  year={{{year}}},\\\\n  booktitle={{{venue}}},\\\\n  url={{{url}}}\\\\n}}\\\\n\\\\n'\nfor key,title,author,url in [('qwenbase','{Qwen2.5-1.5B} model card','Qwen Team','https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B'),('qweninstruct','{Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct} model card','Qwen Team','https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct'),('detectorcard','{ChatGPT} detector {RoBERTa}: Model card','Hello-SimpleAI','https://huggingface.co/Hello-SimpleAI/chatgpt-detector-roberta'),('openrouter','Model catalog {API}','OpenRouter','https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/models')]:\n bib+=f'@misc{{{key},\\\\n  title={{{title}}},\\\\n  author={{{{{author}}}}},\\\\n  howpublished={{\\\\\\\\url{{{url}}}}},\\\\n  note={{Accessed 2026-10-04; historical snapshot recorded with experiment artifacts}}\\\\n}}\\\\n\\\\n'\nw('references.bib',bib)\nPY\npython /tmp/write_appendix.py\ncd paper_draft && pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode main.tex > build-pass1.log && bibtex main > build-bibtex.log && pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode main.tex > build-pass2.log && pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode main.tex > build-pass3.log\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat > /tmp/write_appendix.py <<'PY'\nexec(Path('/tmp/write_paper.py').read_text().split(\\\"w('main.tex'\\\")[0]) if False else None\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport csv,json\np=Path('paper_draft')\ndef w(n,s):(p/n).write_text(s.strip()+'\\\\n')\nw('appendix/reproducibility.tex',r'''\n\\\\section{Reproducibility and Execution Details}\\\\label{sec:reproduce}\n\\\\subsection{Frozen models and environment}\nThe research execution date was 2026-10-04. The local checkpoints and immutable revisions were:\n\\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=2pt,topsep=2pt]\n\\\\item \\\\texttt{Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct}:\\\\\\\\\\\\texttt{989aa7980e4cf806f80c7fef2b1adb7bc71aa306}.\n\\\\item \\\\texttt{Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B}:\\\\\\\\\\\\texttt{8faed761d45a263340a0528343f099c05c9a4323}.\n\\\\item \\\\texttt{Hello-SimpleAI/chatgpt-detector-roberta}:\\\\\\\\\\\\texttt{d2b342c61775d5dd0221808a79983ed3b86ffd86}.\n\\\\end{itemize}\nThe external judge alias was \\\\texttt{google/gemini-3.8-flash}; the archived catalog canonical slug was \\\\texttt{google/gemini-3.8-flash-20260902}, and the returned provider was Google. Catalog parameters and pricing were checked before judge selection. Requests used the OpenRouter chat-completions endpoint, default routing, temperature 0, JSON output, and \\\\texttt{max\\\\_tokens=3000}. The catalog snapshot records historical selection rather than guaranteeing immutable API behavior \\\\cite{openrouter}.\n\nOne NVIDIA RTX A6000 (49,140 MiB) supported local inference. The project environment used Python 3.12.8, PyTorch 2.14.1+cu130, Transformers 4.57.6, NumPy 2.5.3, scikit-learn 1.9.1, and SciPy 1.18.1. Inference used bfloat16, SDPA, extraction batches of 32, and generation batches of 16. Peak allocated memory was 16.1 GB per process. Instruction and base pipelines took 462.7 and 175.4 seconds and partly overlapped; these are not training times or a controlled speed comparison. Native JIT was disabled with \\\\texttt{TORCH\\\\_DISABLE\\\\_NATIVE\\\\_JIT=1} after an initial failure due to a missing C compiler.\n\n\\\\subsection{Rating rubric and prompt specification}\nThe judge system instruction defined AI-likeness as how strongly English prose sounds like generic machine writing, explicitly excluding proof of authorship. Coherence anchors were 1: unintelligible; 2: major problems; 3: understandable but disjointed; 4: clear with minor awkwardness; and 5: fluent coherent prose. Coverage measured the fraction of supplied source facts accurately preserved. Unsupported claims measured the fraction of factual claims absent from or contradicting the source. For source-free calibration items, coverage and unsupported values were fixed by the rubric to 1 and 0. The rubric requested sensitivity to repetition, abrupt endings, contradictions, and omission; formality alone did not imply machine authorship, and casual style alone did not merit reward. Texts were to be treated as data rather than instructions. Exact wording and JSON schema are stored in \\\\path{results/judge_rubric.json}.\n\nThe standard generation instruction requested one clear 70--100-word announcement paragraph, preservation of all supplied facts, no additional factual claims, and only the paragraph as output. One source record described a library reading workshop, coordinator, time, item count, free attendance, and a deadline two days before the event. Complete records and prompts accompany every generated output. Separate pottery-course records supplied nuisance prompts; no nuisance output was counted among the 1,472 task outputs.\n\n\\\\subsection{Pairing, inference, and selected subsets}\nFor prompt \"'$i$, let $d_i$ be the treatment-minus-zero score difference after averaging available paired seeds. The primary point estimate is $64''^{-1}'\"\\\\sum_i d_i\"'$; the percentile bootstrap resamples prompt indices while preserving their within-prompt pairing. A paired sign flip multiplies each $d_i$ by an independently sampled sign. The Monte Carlo two-sided probability uses an add-one correction over 10,000 draws. Only the two primary external-style tests receive Holm adjustment. The exploratory condition comparisons retain all available paired outputs, including failures.\n\nThe matched-quality analysis uses seed 42 pairs with coherence at least 4 in both conditions, absolute coherence difference at most 0.25, and coverage at least 0.95 in both conditions. The instruction mean $-2$ subset has 11 pairs and the base counterpart has 22. These filters use treatment outcomes and do not estimate population preservation. Likewise, length-adjusted regression may remove a mediator of the intervention. We report these checks only descriptively.\n\n'\"\\\\subsection{Deviations from the aspirational handoff}\\\\label{sec:deviations}\nThe frozen feasible protocol reduced the original target of at least 300 prompts, three seeds, and multiple model families to 64 records, two primary seeds, and paired checkpoints from one family. It used last-token states at three layers, not an exhaustive layer/position study. No SAE analysis, shuffled-label intervention, norm-matched random residualized control, crossed persona/style prompt experiment, dedicated fluency intervention, or full formality-manipulation validation was run. Verbosity contributed to the removed nuisance span but was not a standalone steering condition. We did not run full \\\\textsc{RAID}/\\\\textsc{MAGE} stress tests, an independent likelihood detector, or causal generation interventions on natural \\\\hape continuations. These omissions are not claimed as completed experiments. Base controls beyond signed doses were not run. The full inference pipeline was not rerun twice; repeated smoke batches and a repeated complete numerical analysis were checked.\n\n\\\\subsection{Artifacts, checks, and cost}\nThe project README supplies execution commands. Implementations are \\\\path{src/prepare.py}, \\\\path{src/experiment.py}, \\\\path{src/score.py}, \\\\path{src/analyze.py}, \\\\path{src/audit_analysis.py}, \\\\path{src/supplement.py}, and \\\\path{src/validate.py}. \\\\path{results/corpus.json} records source IDs, excerpts, labels, and hashes; original resource manifests record data revisions. \\\\path{results/config.json} records configuration, and \\\\path{results/tasks.json} plus \\\\path{results/generations.jsonl} preserve full prompts and outputs. Native activations and vectors reside in \\\\path{results/*.npz}; \\\\path{pyproject.toml} and \\\\path{uv.lock} pin dependencies. \\\\path{results/validation.json} records complete scoring, disjoint folds, hook and sampling checks, and identical repeated numerical-analysis hashes. These artifacts support cached reproducibility without claiming deterministic future API responses.\n\nThere were 208 successful API requests, including calibration and repeated ratings, with 282,947 prompt tokens and 109,513 completion tokens. Returned usage cost totaled \\\\\"'$0.622884, excluding unmonetized local inference. No ultimately failed calls were present. Equal allocation of mixed-batch costs among items gives approximate per-condition costs; instruction mean $-2$, including its share of re-ratings, cost '\"\\\\\"'$0.0598. Full cached responses omit credentials.\n'\"''')\nw('appendix/extended_results.tex',r'''\n\\\\section{Extended Results and Error Analysis}\\\\label{sec:extended}\n\\\\subsection{Complete control intervals}\n\\\\Figref{fig:controls} shows marginal 95\\\\% prompt-bootstrap intervals for instruction controls. \\\\Tabref{tab:extendedstyle} and \\\\tabref{tab:extendedquality} list all individual conditions, including each random direction and the base signed doses, directly from the cached paired-comparison table. Style tests outside the two primary instruction mean \"'$-2$ contrasts are exploratory; the plotted intervals are not simultaneous across conditions.\n'\"\\\\begin{figure}[ht]\\\\centering\n\\\\includegraphics[width=0.95\\\\linewidth]{figures/controls.png}\n\\\\caption{Instruction-model control contrasts relative to paired zero outputs. Points and bars show mean changes and marginal prompt-bootstrap 95\\\\% CIs. Mean \"'$'\"\\\\pm2\"'$ uses two seeds per prompt; other conditions use seed 42. The large negative judged effect of mean $-2$ accompanies coherence loss and is not corroborated by the encoder.}'\"\\\\label{fig:controls}\n\\\\end{figure}\n\\\\input{tables/extended_style}\n\\\\input{tables/extended_quality}\n\\\\clearpage\n\\\\subsection{Readout transfer visualization}\n\\\\begin{figure}[ht]\\\\centering\n\\\\includegraphics[width=0.95\\\\linewidth]{figures/readout.png}\n\\\\caption{Validation-selected mean-direction readout AUROC by backbone and held-out setting. Each generator-setting uses 128 human/machine source pairs; human references are shared within each source set. The dashed line denotes chance AUROC. The plot shows point estimates; intervals and logistic alternatives are cached with the readout results. Transfer to Llama base is much weaker than transfer to instruction-generated text.}\\\\label{fig:readout}\n\\\\end{figure}\n\n\\\\subsection{Observed failures}\nThe instruction baseline already has mean unsupported-claim fraction 0.179, and only 14.8\\\\% of its outputs meet the requested 70--100-word range. Under mean \"'$-2$, the proportion rated below coherence 4 rises from 5.5'\"\\\\% to 21.9\\\\%. Under mean \"'$+2$, detected Chinese/Cyrillic-script mixing occurs in 3.9'\"\\\\% of outputs, versus 1.6\\\\% at zero, and truncation occurs in 2.3\\\\%. These are descriptive flags, not grounds for removing outputs.\n\nIn the fixed first-library-task comparison recorded in the report, zero invents an anniversary; mean \"'$-2$ invents an end time and duration and calls ``13:00'\"'' noon; mean \"'$+2$ adds ornate promotion and a Chinese ending. The example was fixed by prompt order rather than selected for favorable steering. Other observed failures include invented target audiences or refreshments, omitted registration deadlines, exaggerated claims, and awkward quantity descriptions. Full paired text and lowest-rated outputs are preserved in '\"\\\\path{results/paired_examples.json} and \\\\path{results/failure_examples.json}. These errors explain why source-fact coverage alone is inadequate: an output can retain supplied facts while adding false claims.\n''')\nrows=list(csv.DictReader(open('results/paired_comparisons.csv')))\nlabels={'ablate':'Ablation','assistant_m2':'Assistant \"'$-2$'\"','formality_m2':'Formality \"'$-2$'\"','human_prompt':'Human prompt','logistic_m2':'Logistic \"'$-2$'\"','mean_m1':'Mean \"'$-1$'\"','mean_m2':'Mean \"'$-2$'\"','mean_p1':'Mean \"'$+1$'\"','mean_p2':'Mean \"'$+2$'\"','residual_m2':'Residualized \"'$-2$'\"','residual_p2':'Residualized \"'$+2$'\"'}\nfor i in range(5):labels['random'+str(i)]='Random '+str(i+1)\nfor name,metrics,heads in [('style',['ai_likeness','encoder'],['Judge AI-likeness','Encoder probability']),('quality',['coherence','content','unsupported'],['Coherence','Coverage','Unsupported'])]:\n s=r'\\\\begin{table}[p]\\\\centering\\\\scriptsize'+'\\\\n'+r'\\\\caption{All paired '+name+r' changes [95\\\\% CI], 64 records per row. Instruction mean \"'$'\"\\\\pm2\"'$ uses two seeds; other rows use seed 42. Comparisons use matching zero seeds.}'\"\\\\label{tab:extended'+name+'}\\\\n'+r'\\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{\"'!}{'\"\\\\begin{tabular}{@{}ll'+'l'*len(metrics)+'@{}}\\\\n\\\\\\\\toprule\\\\nBackbone & Condition & '+' & '.join(heads)+r'\\\\\\\\\\\\midrule'+'\\\\n'\n for model in ['instruct','base']:\n  for cond in dict.fromkeys(r['condition'] for r in rows if r['model']==model):\n   cells=[]\n   for met in metrics:\n    r=next(r for r in rows if r['model']==model and r['condition']==cond and r['metric']==met)\n    cells.append('\"'$'\"'+f\\\"{float(r['delta']):+.3f}\\\\\\\\;[{float(r['lo']):+.3f},{float(r['hi']):+.3f}]\\\"+'\"'$'\"')\n   s+=('Instruction' if model=='instruct' else 'Base')+' & '+labels[cond]+' & '+' & '.join(cells)+r'\\\\\\\\'+'\\\\n'\n s+='\\\\\\\\bottomrule\\\\n\\\\\\\\end{tabular}}\\\\n\\\\\\\\end{table}\\\\n'\n w('tables/extended_'+name+'.tex',s)\nrefs=[\n('quaremba2026','Linear Probing Provides Robust and Efficient Detection of Machine-Generated Text','Quaremba, Gerrit and Yan, Hanqi and Black, Elizabeth and Vrandečić, Denny and Simperl, Elena','2026','2608.24780'),\n('vishnyakov2026','{SV-Detect}: {AI}-generated Text Detection with Steering Vectors','Vishnyakov, Mikhail and Gaintseva, Tatiana','2026','2606.07313'),\n('kuznetsov2025','Feature-Level Insights into Artificial Text Detection with Sparse Autoencoders','Kuznetsov, Kristian and Kushnareva, Laida and Druzhinina, Polina and Razzhigaev, Anton and Voznyuk, Anastasia and Piontkovskaya, Irina and Burnaev, Evgeny and Barannikov, Serguei','2025','2503.03601'),\n('lu2026','The Assistant Axis: Situating and Stabilizing the Default Persona of Language Models','Lu, Christina and Gallagher, Jack and Michala, Jonathan and Fish, Kyle and Lindsey, Jack','2026','2601.10387'),\n('reinhart2025','Do {LLMs} write like humans? Variation in grammatical and rhetorical styles','Reinhart, Alex and Markey, Ben and Laudenbach, Michael and Pantusen, Kachatad and Yurko, Ronald and Weinberg, Gordon and Brown, David West','2025','2410.16107'),\n('guo2023','How Close is {ChatGPT} to Human Experts? Comparison Corpus, Evaluation, and Detection','Guo, Biyang and Zhang, Xin and Wang, Ziyuan and Jiang, Minqi and Nie, Jinran and Ding, Yuxuan and Yue, Jianwei and Wu, Yupeng','2023','2301.07597')]\nbib=''\nfor key,title,author,year,aid in refs:\n author=author.replace('Vrandečić',r'Vrande\\\\v{c}i\\\\'{c}')\n bib+=f'@article{{{key},\\\\n  title={{{title}}},\\\\n  author={{{author}}},\\\\n  year={{{year}}},\\\\n  journal={{arXiv preprint arXiv:{aid}}},\\\\n  url={{https://arxiv.org/abs/{aid}}}\\\\n}}\\\\n\\\\n'\nfor key,title,author,year,venue,url in [\n('rimsky2024','Steering {Llama 2} via Contrastive Activation Addition','Rimsky, Nina and Gabrieli, Nick and Schulz, Julian and Tong, Meg and Hubinger, Evan and Turner, Alexander Matt','2024','Proceedings of the 62nd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers)','https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.828/'),\n('dugan2024','{RAID}: A Shared Benchmark for Robust Evaluation of Machine-Generated Text Detectors','Dugan, Liam and Hwang, Alyssa and Trhlik, Filip and Ludan, Josh Magnus and Zhu, Andrew and Xu, Hainiu and Ippolito, Daphne and Callison-Burch, Chris','2024','Proceedings of the 62nd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics','https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.07940'),\n('li2024','{MAGE}: Machine-generated Text Detection in the Wild','Li, Yafu and Li, Qintong and Cui, Leyang and Bi, Wei and Wang, Zhilin and Wang, Longyue and Yang, Linyi and Shi, Shuming and Zhang, Yue','2024','Proceedings of the 62nd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics','https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.13242'),\n('bao2024','{Fast-DetectGPT}: Efficient Zero-Shot Detection of Machine-Generated Text via Conditional Probability Curvature','Bao, Guangsheng and Zhao, Yanbin and Teng, Zhiyang and Yang, Linyi and Zhang, Yue','2024','International Conference on Learning Representations','https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.05130')]:\n bib+=f'@inproceedings{{{key},\\\\n  title={{{title}}},\\\\n  author={{{author}}},\\\\n  year={{{year}}},\\\\n  booktitle={{{venue}}},\\\\n  url={{{url}}}\\\\n}}\\\\n\\\\n'\nfor key,title,author,url in [('qwenbase','{Qwen2.5-1.5B} model card','Qwen Team','https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B'),('qweninstruct','{Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct} model card','Qwen Team','https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct'),('detectorcard','{ChatGPT} detector {RoBERTa}: Model card','Hello-SimpleAI','https://huggingface.co/Hello-SimpleAI/chatgpt-detector-roberta'),('openrouter','Model catalog {API}','OpenRouter','https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/models')]:\n bib+=f'@misc{{{key},\\\\n  title={{{title}}},\\\\n  author={{{{{author}}}}},\\\\n  howpublished={{\\\\\\\\url{{{url}}}}},\\\\n  note={{Accessed 2026-10-04; historical snapshot recorded with experiment artifacts}}\\\\n}}\\\\n\\\\n'\nw('references.bib',bib)\nPY\npython /tmp/write_appendix.py\ncd paper_draft && pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode main.tex > build-pass1.log && bibtex main > build-bibtex.log && pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode main.tex > build-pass2.log && pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode main.tex > build-pass3.log\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
